  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nSaturday night through Monday, appreciable rainfall looks likely. \n\nSaturday night...The main upper trough and closed low will advance \nslowly east across the Central Rockies and Four Corners. The cold \nfront is forecast to stall out across far southwest Nebraska through \nnortheast Nebraska through Sunday. As the upper trough/closed low \nmoves very slowly east into northeast Wyoming and Colorado by late \nSunday night, showers chances are categorical. NBM 48 hour \nprobability of 0.50 inch or greater ending 12Z Tuesday is 75 to 95 \npercent, with highest probabilities from Hayes Center through North \nPlatte and Ainsworth. Noteworthy, that greater than 1.0 inch for \nthis same corridor at 75 to 85 percent. The 12Z GEFS ensemble QPF \ncontinues to increase from previous runs, now showing rainfall \namounts above 1.0 inch east of Valentine through Big Springs,\nwith amounts exceeding 1.75 inches southwest into central\nNebraska. The ECMWF is further west, with amounts above 1.0 \ninch, with highest amounts near 1.5 inches near the Highway 83 \ncorridor. Lows 35 to around 40 Saturday night. Soundings in the \nnorthwest Sandhills show any precipitation remaining as rain. \nHighs Sunday 45 to 55 with an east to northeast wind 10 to 15 \nmph. Could see a mix of rain/snow Sunday night across the \nnorthwest Sandhills as H85 temperatures fall below 0C. \n\nMonday into Monday night...The upper trough will cross Nebraska and \nmay develop a closed low over central into eastern Nebraska. Likely \nto categorical POPs Monday, then lowering to chance pops later \nMonday night. Colder air drawn in behind the system could bring a \nmix of rain/snow to north central Nebraska. No accumulations \nexpected at this time, though will monitor model trends and \nsoundings closely. \n\nTuesday through Thursday...Weak upper ridging should gradually \nreturn. Highs in the 50s Tuesday should moderate to the upper 50s to \nlow 60s Wednesday and Thursday. Lingering light showers \npossible Tuesday into Tuesday night.",
